-- Lesson 3: Linguistic and Cushitic Roots
UPDATE public.lessons SET content = $CONTENT$## A Living Cushitic Language

Afaan Oromoo belongs to the **East Cushitic** branch of the Afroasiatic family. With around 40 million speakers, it is the most widely spoken Cushitic language and among the top languages of Africa by population.

| Branch | Examples |
|--------|----------|
| North Cushitic | Beja |
| Central Cushitic | Agaw languages |
| East Cushitic | Oromo, Somali, Afar, Sidama |
| South Cushitic | Iraqw (in Tanzania) |

## Shared Features Across Cushitic

- SOV word order
- Rich case marking on nouns
- Gender distinctions (masculine/feminine)
- Focus particles
- Aspect-prominent verbs

## Relationship to Other Families

Cushitic is a sister branch of Semitic (Amharic, Arabic, Hebrew) within Afroasiatic. Though Oromo and Amharic share deep ancestry, they are different languages with different structures.

## Importance

Linguistic classification matters: it places the Oromo within a long African continuum and rebuts old claims that the Oromo were "late arrivals" in the region.

**Key takeaway:** Afaan Oromoo is a major East Cushitic language with ancient African roots and over 40 million speakers.
$CONTENT$
