-- Lesson 3: Building Credit
UPDATE public.lessons SET content = $CONTENT$## Why Credit Matters in the US

In the United States, your **credit score** affects loan approval, interest rates, apartment applications, car insurance pricing, and sometimes employment. For newcomers and first-generation Americans, building credit from zero is a rite of passage.

## The FICO Score

| Range | Rating |
|-------|--------|
| 800-850 | Exceptional |
| 740-799 | Very Good |
| 670-739 | Good |
| 580-669 | Fair |
| 300-579 | Poor |

## What Builds Credit

| Factor | Weight |
|--------|--------|
| Payment history | 35% |
| Amounts owed | 30% |
| Length of history | 15% |
| New credit | 10% |
| Credit mix | 10% |

## Starting from Zero

1. **Secured credit card** — small deposit becomes your limit; responsible use builds history.
2. **Credit-builder loan** — small loan held in savings; payments reported to bureaus.
3. **Authorized user** — on a trusted family member's card.
4. **Student credit card** — if enrolled.

## Golden Rules

- Pay on time, every time. Autopay the minimum.
- Keep utilization under 30% of limit (under 10% is best).
- Don't close your oldest card.
- Check your credit reports free annually at AnnualCreditReport.com.

**Practice tip:** Open one secured card, charge only a small recurring bill, and autopay it in full monthly. Watch your score climb.
$CONTENT$
